(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','HOURS CHANGE — Beginning November 1, the Main Library closes at 9:00 p.m. on weekdays instead of 10:00 p.m. Weekend hours do not change. The second-floor reading room stays open around the clock for currently enrolled students.','NONE','["Weekday closing becomes earlier.","Weekend closing becomes earlier.","The reading room closes at 9:00 p.m.","Only faculty may use the reading room."]','A',1,'Only weekday hours change, from 10:00 p.m. to 9:00 p.m.; the reading room remains open 24 hours for students.',16),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Ana: "Correction — the field trip bus leaves at 7:40, not 8:00, and from gate 3 instead of gate 1." — Ben: "Thanks, I will pass that on before morning roll call."','NONE','["The bus leaves earlier and from another gate.","The trip has been cancelled.","The bus leaves later from the same gate.","Roll call has been moved to the bus."]','A',1,'Both changes are time (7:40 instead of 8:00) and gate (3 instead of 1).',17),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','PROFESSOR EMAIL — Because the science building will be closed for electrical work all weekend, the problem set that was due Friday will now be accepted until Monday at noon. Submit online; no paper copy is needed.','NONE','["The deadline moved because the building is closed.","The assignment was cancelled.","The assignment must be printed.","The deadline moved to Friday afternoon."]','A',1,'The weekend closure caused the extension to Monday noon, and online submission is required.',18),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign-up page and answer the question.','PEER TUTORING — Each session lasts 45 minutes, and a student may book two sessions per week. Cancel at least 12 hours ahead; a later cancellation still counts as a used session.','NONE','["A cancellation made six hours ahead uses up one session.","A student may book four sessions per week.","Sessions last one hour.","Cancellations are never allowed."]','A',1,'Cancelling inside the 12-hour window still counts the session as used; the weekly limit is two and the length is 45 minutes.',19),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the instructions and answer the question.','DEPOSIT SLIP — Write your student number in box 1 and your family name in box 2. Do not sign the slip until the clerk hands it back to you at the window.','NONE','["Sign only after the clerk returns the slip.","Sign the slip before filling in any box.","Write your family name in box 1.","Give the signed slip to the clerk before queuing."]','A',1,'The instruction explicitly delays signing until the clerk returns the slip; box 1 is the student number.',20),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the dormitory notice and answer the question.','DORMITORY NOTICE — The elevator in Hall B will be out of service on Saturday from 8:00 a.m. to noon for cable inspection. Residents on floors 4 and above should carry rubbish downstairs before Friday evening.','NONE','["Higher-floor residents should move rubbish before the weekend shutdown.","The elevator will be closed all weekend.","All students must move out on Saturday.","The inspection will take two days."]','A',1,'The shutdown is Saturday morning only, so the notice asks upper-floor residents to clear rubbish by Friday evening.',21),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the lab rule and answer the question.','CHEMISTRY LAB SAFETY — Safety goggles must be worn at all times inside the lab, even when no experiment is running. Visitors must sign the register mounted by the door before entering.','NONE','["Goggles are required even between experiments.","Goggles are needed only during experiments.","Visitors may skip the register.","Visitors must wear goggles for one hour."]','A',1,'The rule requires goggles continuously, and visitors must sign the register first.',22),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the messages and answer the question.','Ji-woo: "I recorded Thursday''s lecture, but my battery died, so the final ten minutes are missing." — Omar: "No problem — the professor posted the slides, and the last slide has the homework."','NONE','["The homework on the posted slides","A second recording from Ji-woo","A repeat of the lecture","The textbook chapter"]','A',1,'Omar says the slides are posted and that the final slide contains the homework, so the missing audio is not a problem.',23),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the campus email and answer the question.','PARKING OFFICE — Your permit application was received, but it cannot be processed while a $25 registration hold remains on your account. Once the hold is cleared, allow three business days for the permit to arrive by mail.','NONE','["Clearing a charge on the account","Waiting three business days to apply","Printing the permit at the office","Cancelling the application"]','A',1,'Processing is blocked by the registration hold; the three-day wait comes after the hold is cleared.',24),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the menu card and answer the question.','CAFE MENU — Lentil soup is served today. A vegetarian main dish appears every day of the week, while the second hot main changes daily. Sandwiches are available only before 2:00 p.m.','NONE','["The vegetarian main dish","The sandwich board","The second hot main","The lentil soup"]','A',1,'Only the vegetarian main is promised on every day; the soup is today and the second hot main rotates.',25),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the alert and answer the question.','DISTRICT ALERT — Friday school buses will run two hours behind schedule because roads will stay frozen until mid-morning. After-school activities are cancelled, but the building remains open until 6:00 p.m. for students whose families cannot rearrange childcare.','NONE','["Classes start later and evening activities are called off.","Classes end earlier and buses run normally.","The school is closed all day Friday.","Childcare will be provided until 8:00 p.m."]','A',1,'Buses and therefore the day start shift two hours later, and activities are cancelled; the building still closes at 6:00 p.m.',26),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the library slip and answer the question.','RENEWAL TERMS — Renew an item by phone, online, or at the desk before its due date. An item that another borrower has requested may be renewed only once.','NONE','["A second renewal is blocked if someone else requested the item.","Renewals are possible only online.","Requested items must be returned immediately.","The due date extends automatically."]','A',1,'Once another borrower requests an item, the single renewal is used up.',27),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the workshop flyer and answer the question.','DIGITAL DESIGN WORKSHOP — Bring your own laptop. Ten loaner machines are available at the door on a first-come basis and must be returned before the lunch break.','NONE','["Only the first ten attendees may borrow a machine for the morning","Every attendee will be given a laptop","Laptops are forbidden in the workshop","Loaners may be kept until the end of the day"]','A',1,'Loaners are limited to ten, given first-come, and must be returned before lunch.',28),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the group chat and answer the question.','Priya: "Our presentation slot moved to 2:30 p.m., so we will rehearse at 11:00 instead of 1:00." — Ravi: "Then I will bring the clicker in the morning."','NONE','["11:00 a.m.","1:00 p.m.","2:30 p.m.","8:00 a.m."]','A',1,'The rehearsal moved to 11:00 a.m.; 2:30 p.m. is the presentation itself.',29),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the health centre notice and answer the question.','HEALTH CENTRE — Flu shots are given walk-in on Tuesdays and Thursdays from 1:00 to 4:00 p.m. while supplies last. Students with any allergy should speak with the nurse at the front desk first.','NONE','["Check with a nurse before the shot if there is an allergy","Book an appointment weeks ahead","Arrive on Friday morning","Bring a doctor''s note"]','A',1,'Allergic students are directed to the nurse before vaccination; no appointment is needed and supply may run out.',30),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the registrar email and answer the question.','REGISTRAR — Your transfer credit request is complete. Evaluation takes up to four weeks in the fall term, so please do not enrol in courses above the 200 level until you receive the result.','NONE','["Enrolment in advanced courses waits for the evaluation","The request must be sent again","Evaluation is finished within a week","Only 100-level credits will be accepted"]','A',1,'The four-week evaluation delays enrolment above the 200 level.',31),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the club flyer and answer the question.','ROBOTICS TEAM — Meetings are Wednesday evenings in Engineering B12. The beginner track assumes no prior experience, but every new member must attend one of the two tryout workshops held in September.','NONE','["Attending a September tryout workshop","Prior building experience","A Wednesday evening interview","A written test in Engineering B12"]','A',1,'Experience is not required; the only stated condition is one September tryout workshop.',32),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the gallery card and answer the question.','GALLERY CARD — The exhibition hall is closed on Mondays in July and August it is open every day. Photographing costs $10 per room and the licence must be bought at the desk before you enter; flash is never permitted.','NONE','["A Monday in August","A Monday in November","Any day except July","Only with a school ID"]','A',1,'July and August are the exceptions to the Monday closure, so a Monday in August is open.',33),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the shelter sign and answer the question.','ROUTE 12 — Buses leave every 20 minutes until 8:00 p.m. and every 45 minutes afterward. The final trip toward campus departs the station at 10:40 p.m.','NONE','["Up to 45 minutes","About 20 minutes","Exactly one hour","No bus will run"]','A',1,'After 8:00 p.m. the interval widens to 45 minutes, and the 10:40 p.m. trip still exists.',34),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','OFFICE HOURS — This week my hours are online. Send a one-paragraph summary of your draft at least 24 hours before your slot, or the slot is released to the waiting list.','NONE','["Not sending the summary a day early","Arriving without a laptop","Scheduling two meetings","Paying the online fee"]','A',1,'The slot goes to the waitlist unless the summary arrives 24 hours in advance.',35),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','Many cities stay several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside after sunset. The effect, called the urban heat island, appears because pavement and dark roofs absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night, while vegetation cools the air by evaporating water through its leaves. Tall buildings add to the problem by trapping warmed air at street level and slowing the wind that would carry it away. Planners now test two inexpensive remedies: coating roofs and roads with reflective paint, and restoring tree canopy above busy intersections. Measurements in several mid-sized cities suggest that reflective coatings cool a street by about one degree, whereas mature trees lower the air beneath them by three degrees or more while also shading the surfaces that store heat.','NONE','["Why cities stay hot at night and how two remedies compare","How to design a tall building in a warm climate","Why trees should be removed from busy intersections","How much pavement costs in a mid-sized city"]','A',1,'The passage names a cause (heat-storing surfaces, trapped air) and then compares two remedies and their measured effects.',36),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "trapping" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Many cities stay several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside after sunset. The effect, called the urban heat island, appears because pavement and dark roofs absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night, while vegetation cools the air by evaporating water through its leaves. Tall buildings add to the problem by trapping warmed air at street level and slowing the wind that would carry it away. Planners now test two inexpensive remedies: coating roofs and roads with reflective paint, and restoring tree canopy above busy intersections. Measurements in several mid-sized cities suggest that reflective coatings cool a street by about one degree, whereas mature trees lower the air beneath them by three degrees or more while also shading the surfaces that store heat.','NONE','["letting it escape","holding it in place","measuring it","hiding it"]','B',1,'Buildings keep warm air at street level instead of letting wind carry it off, so "trapping" = holding in place.',37),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why do mature trees cool a street more than reflective coatings?','Many cities stay several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside after sunset. The effect, called the urban heat island, appears because pavement and dark roofs absorb sunlight during the day and release it slowly at night, while vegetation cools the air by evaporating water through its leaves. Tall buildings add to the problem by trapping warmed air at street level and slowing the wind that would carry it away. Planners now test two inexpensive remedies: coating roofs and roads with reflective paint, and restoring tree canopy above busy intersections. Measurements in several mid-sized cities suggest that reflective coatings cool a street by about one degree, whereas mature trees lower the air beneath them by three degrees or more while also shading the surfaces that store heat.','NONE','["Trees both cool the air above them and shade the surfaces that store heat.","Reflective coatings are illegal in most cities.","Trees work only after sunset.","Coatings slow the wind that would carry heat away."]','A',1,'The passage gives trees a larger measured drop (three degrees or more) and adds that they also shade heat-storing surfaces.',38),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','In the dance, the vertical angle of the central run indicates:','Honey bees that return from a rich food source perform a figure-eight dance on the vertical comb, and the dance carries directions. The vertical angle of the central run stands for the angle away from the sun: a bee running straight up the comb signals food in the direction of the sun, while a run 60 degrees to the right means the field lies 60 degrees right of the sun. The length and tempo of the run report distance, a slower and longer run describing a farther field. Workers that watch the dance leave the hive and fly the announced bearing, often finding the patch within a few metres of where the dancer pointed. Because gravity replaces the sun as the reference on a vertical wall, the same movement works in a dark hive.','NONE','["how far the food is","the direction of the food relative to the sun","how rich the food is","how many workers already visited"]','B',1,'Angle encodes direction relative to the sun; distance is carried by the length and tempo of the run.',39),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "report" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Honey bees that return from a rich food source perform a figure-eight dance on the vertical comb, and the dance carries directions. The vertical angle of the central run stands for the angle away from the sun: a bee running straight up the comb signals food in the direction of the sun, while a run 60 degrees to the right means the field lies 60 degrees right of the sun. The length and tempo of the run report distance, a slower and longer run describing a farther field. Workers that watch the dance leave the hive and fly the announced bearing, often finding the patch within a few metres of where the dancer pointed. Because gravity replaces the sun as the reference on a vertical wall, the same movement works in a dark hive.','NONE','["argue about","indicate","reduce","repeat"]','B',1,'The run conveys distance information, so "report" = indicate.',40),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about bees dancing inside a dark hive?','Honey bees that return from a rich food source perform a figure-eight dance on the vertical comb, and the dance carries directions. The vertical angle of the central run stands for the angle away from the sun: a bee running straight up the comb signals food in the direction of the sun, while a run 60 degrees to the right means the field lies 60 degrees right of the sun. The length and tempo of the run report distance, a slower and longer run describing a farther field. Workers that watch the dance leave the hive and fly the announced bearing, often finding the patch within a few metres of where the dancer pointed. Because gravity replaces the sun as the reference on a vertical wall, the same movement works in a dark hive.','NONE','["They must wait until daylight to dance.","Their dance still communicates an angle, using gravity as the reference.","Their observers fly toward the nearest light source.","Their run length no longer carries distance."]','B',1,'The final sentence states that gravity substitutes for the sun, so the angle remains meaningful without light.',41),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why are the deepest parts of an ice core the oldest?','Drilling through an Antarctic ice sheet produces a cylinder of ice that is a readable record of past climates. Snow that falls each year is buried, compressed, and turned to ice, so the deepest sections are the oldest; seasonal swings in dust and crystal size let researchers count annual layers the way a forester counts tree rings. Air bubbles trapped inside preserve samples of the ancient atmosphere, and measuring the carbon dioxide within them shows how much of the gas existed when the snow fell. Below a few hundred metres, layering disappears and dating depends on models of how ice flows, which widens the uncertainty. The oldest recovered cores run close to a million years, and the carbon dioxide record they hold matches temperature estimates drawn from a separate isotope of hydrogen in the ice itself.','NONE','["Because later snowfall covers each year''s snow and presses it down","Because ice at the bottom melts more slowly","Because researchers place older samples at the bottom","Because dust sinks through the sheet"]','A',1,'Each year''s snow is buried by the snow that follows it, so depth corresponds to age.',42),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The phrase "drawn from" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Drilling through an Antarctic ice sheet produces a cylinder of ice that is a readable record of past climates. Snow that falls each year is buried, compressed, and turned to ice, so the deepest sections are the oldest; seasonal swings in dust and crystal size let researchers count annual layers the way a forester counts tree rings. Air bubbles trapped inside preserve samples of the ancient atmosphere, and measuring the carbon dioxide within them shows how much of the gas existed when the snow fell. Below a few hundred metres, layering disappears and dating depends on models of how ice flows, which widens the uncertainty. The oldest recovered cores run close to a million years, and the carbon dioxide record they hold matches temperature estimates drawn from a separate isotope of hydrogen in the ice itself.','NONE','["taken from","removed from","far from","learnt from"]','A',1,'The estimates are derived from the hydrogen isotope, so "drawn from" = taken from.',43),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What limits the accuracy of dating deep sections of a core?','Drilling through an Antarctic ice sheet produces a cylinder of ice that is a readable record of past climates. Snow that falls each year is buried, compressed, and turned to ice, so the deepest sections are the oldest; seasonal swings in dust and crystal size let researchers count annual layers the way a forester counts tree rings. Air bubbles trapped inside preserve samples of the ancient atmosphere, and measuring the carbon dioxide within them shows how much of the gas existed when the snow fell. Below a few hundred metres, layering disappears and dating depends on models of how ice flows, which widens the uncertainty. The oldest recovered cores run close to a million years, and the carbon dioxide record they hold matches temperature estimates drawn from a separate isotope of hydrogen in the ice itself.','NONE','["Visible annual layers are no longer preserved, so models of ice flow must be used.","Deep ice contains no air bubbles at all.","Drilling destroys the carbon dioxide.","Temperature estimates are impossible in Antarctic ice."]','A',1,'The passage says layering disappears below a few hundred metres and dating then relies on flow models, increasing uncertainty.',44),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main purpose of the passage?','For decades researchers reported that people who use two languages every day outperform monolinguals at tasks requiring them to ignore irrelevant information, an advantage said to come from constant practice in selecting one language while holding the other quiet. Larger and better designed studies have weakened that claim. When the two groups are matched for income, immigration history, and education, much of the difference shrinks or disappears, and studies with hundreds of participants often find no advantage at all. One plausible account of the earlier pattern is that bilingual samples have tended to include more recent immigrants, whose daily need to negotiate unfamiliar systems may itself sharpen attention. The debate matters beyond language: it shows how a group difference can look cognitive when it is partly social, and why matching comparison groups is the first question to ask of any such result.','NONE','["To show how a once-popular claim has been questioned","To argue that learning a second language wastes study time","To describe the daily life of recent immigrants","To prove that attention cannot be measured"]','A',1,'The passage traces a claim from its popularity to the newer, better-matched evidence that weakens it.',45),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "shrinks" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','For decades researchers reported that people who use two languages every day outperform monolinguals at tasks requiring them to ignore irrelevant information, an advantage said to come from constant practice in selecting one language while holding the other quiet. Larger and better designed studies have weakened that claim. When the two groups are matched for income, immigration history, and education, much of the difference shrinks or disappears, and studies with hundreds of participants often find no advantage at all. One plausible account of the earlier pattern is that bilingual samples have tended to include more recent immigrants, whose daily need to negotiate unfamiliar systems may itself sharpen attention. The debate matters beyond language: it shows how a group difference can look cognitive when it is partly social, and why matching comparison groups is the first question to ask of any such result.','NONE','["grows","decreases","settles","survives"]','B',1,'The difference becomes smaller once groups are matched, so "shrinks" = decreases.',46),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about earlier bilingualism studies?','For decades researchers reported that people who use two languages every day outperform monolinguals at tasks requiring them to ignore irrelevant information, an advantage said to come from constant practice in selecting one language while holding the other quiet. Larger and better designed studies have weakened that claim. When the two groups are matched for income, immigration history, and education, much of the difference shrinks or disappears, and studies with hundreds of participants often find no advantage at all. One plausible account of the earlier pattern is that bilingual samples have tended to include more recent immigrants, whose daily need to negotiate unfamiliar systems may itself sharpen attention. The debate matters beyond language: it shows how a group difference can look cognitive when it is partly social, and why matching comparison groups is the first question to ask of any such result.','NONE','["Their comparison groups differed in ways other than language use.","They tested attention too rarely to reach any conclusion.","They measured only reading speed.","Their participants refused to speak two languages."]','A',1,'Because bilingual samples contained more recent immigrants, the groups differed socially as well as linguistically.',47),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','Why was early paper money accepted by its users?','A printed note promises nothing physical. Centuries ago, however, paper money worked because it could be traded for a fixed amount of gold or silver kept in a vault, and holders believed that promise. Modern notes are different: nobody may demand metal for them, and their value rests instead on the fact that a government requires taxes to be paid in the same currency and that prices and wages are quoted in it. Such reliance stays stable only while the supply grows slowly enough for people to expect it to hold. When a government prints to cover spending, prices rise and each note buys less, so holders rush to spend a note before it falls further, which raises prices faster still. History offers many cases in which a currency fell to worthlessness within a decade, and none in which printed promises worked without limits.','NONE','["It could be exchanged for a fixed quantity of metal","It was made of more durable material than coins","It was shorter than later banknotes","It carried the signature of a merchant"]','A',1,'The passage states early notes were redeemable for a set amount of gold or silver held in a vault.',48),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "rests" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','A printed note promises nothing physical. Centuries ago, however, paper money worked because it could be traded for a fixed amount of gold or silver kept in a vault, and holders believed that promise. Modern notes are different: nobody may demand metal for them, and their value rests instead on the fact that a government requires taxes to be paid in the same currency and that prices and wages are quoted in it. Such reliance stays stable only while the supply grows slowly enough for people to expect it to hold. When a government prints to cover spending, prices rise and each note buys less, so holders rush to spend a note before it falls further, which raises prices faster still. History offers many cases in which a currency fell to worthlessness within a decade, and none in which printed promises worked without limits.','NONE','["depends","sleeps","remains","relaxes"]','A',1,'The value is supported by, i.e. depends on, tax requirements and pricing conventions.',49),
(@set02_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why do holders spend a falling note quickly?','A printed note promises nothing physical. Centuries ago, however, paper money worked because it could be traded for a fixed amount of gold or silver kept in a vault, and holders believed that promise. Modern notes are different: nobody may demand metal for them, and their value rests instead on the fact that a government requires taxes to be paid in the same currency and that prices and wages are quoted in it. Such reliance stays stable only while the supply grows slowly enough for people to expect it to hold. When a government prints to cover spending, prices rise and each note buys less, so holders rush to spend a note before it falls further, which raises prices faster still. History offers many cases in which a currency fell to worthlessness within a decade, and none in which printed promises worked without limits.','NONE','["They expect it to lose more value before they use it","They are required by law to spend notes within a week","They can exchange it for metal at any time","They want to buy fewer goods"]','A',1,'The passage describes spending ahead of a further fall, which then accelerates price rises.',50);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Would you mind if I moved my seat closer to the front?','TTS','["Not at all, go ahead.","It is in the third row.","Yes, at three o''clock.","About an hour."]','A',1,'A polite request for permission is answered by granting or refusing it; A grants it.',51),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Has the deadline for the grant application changed again?','TTS','["It was a long form to complete.","Only twice this year, I think.","The office is on the fourth floor.","Send me the file instead."]','B',1,'The question asks how often the deadline changed; B gives a frequency.',52),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you tell me where the returned books are shelved?','TTS','["Right by the east entrance, second aisle.","I returned them yesterday.","The books were overdue.","Yes, I can read them."]','A',1,'A request for a location is answered with directions; A gives the aisle.',53),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Do you think we can finish the model before the demonstration?','TTS','["We should print it later.","Maybe, if we skip the painting step.","It''s made of plastic.","No, the meeting is Thursday."]','B',1,'The question asks for a judgement about finishing in time; B conditions the answer.',54),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Why was the seminar room switched to the hall?','TTS','["Because the projector there needs repair.","At nine in the morning.","By bus, I think.","It was a good seminar."]','A',1,'A "why" question about a change needs a reason; A supplies the broken projector.',55),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How many copies of the handout should I print?','TTS','["One extra is enough.","Thirty, plus five for the tutors.","It''s a two-page handout.","Print it in colour."]','B',1,'A "how many" question needs a quantity; B gives numbers.',56),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','I am thinking of dropping the geology elective. What do you think?','TTS','["The professor gives helpful feedback, so I would keep it.","Geology is about rocks.","She starts at ten.","I dropped lunch instead."]','A',1,'A request for an opinion is answered with a recommendation; A recommends keeping it.',57),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Are the lab coats still in the blue cabinet?','TTS','["They moved them to the green one last month.","Yes, size medium.","No, the lab is closed.","Coats must be buttoned."]','A',1,'A yes/no location question is answered by confirming or correcting the location; A corrects it.',58),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','When does the photograph exhibition close?','TTS','["Next Sunday is the last day.","It was taken in Berlin.","Close to the museum entrance.","About forty photographs."]','A',1,'A "when" question needs a time; A gives the closing day.',59),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Sorry, did you say the tutor is available after four?','TTS','["No, from two until four only.","Yes, she is a tutor.","I said the class is small.","Four students came."]','A',1,'A clarification about availability is answered by correcting the time window.',60),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Which of these data sets did the second group collect?','TTS','["The one on the right, from March.","Yes, they did collect it.","It looks about correct.","Collect the rest later."]','A',1,'A "which" question needs identification of one option; A points to a specific set.',61),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Mind if I record the tutorial for review?','TTS','["Only if you delete it afterwards.","Recording devices are on the shelf.","The tutorial lasts an hour.","It is recorded already."]','A',1,'A permission request is answered by granting it with a condition.',62),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','How much is the deposit for the equipment?','TTS','["Fifty, refunded when you return it.","By credit card only.","It is quite heavy.","Once per semester."]','A',1,'A "how much" question needs an amount; A gives the refundable deposit.',63),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Have you seen the revised room booking policy?','TTS','["Not yet — is it different now?","Yes, room one twenty.","At seven sharp.","Revised by the students."]','A',1,'A yes/no question about having seen something is answered with the status of reading it.',64),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Shall we meet in the cafe or in the library?','TTS','["The library — it is quieter there.","In about ten minutes.","Yes, we met there.","Cafes open late."]','A',1,'An offer of alternatives is answered by choosing one; A chooses with a reason.',65),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','What made you switch from chemistry to materials science?','TTS','["I wanted to work on things that get built.","Second year, I believe.","Chemistry is difficult.","In the old building."]','A',1,'A "what made you" question asks for a motive; A gives the reason for switching.',66),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Is the reading room open during the exam week?','TTS','["It stays open until midnight then.","Yes, it is a long room.","Reading takes practice.","Room seats forty."]','A',1,'A yes/no question about opening hours is confirmed and extended with the detail.',67),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_RESPONSE','Listen to the question and choose the best response.','Could you hold my place while I print the appendix?','TTS','["Sure, I will wave you over when it turns.","The appendix is on page nine.","Place your ID face down.","It prints two sided."]','A',1,'A request to hold a place is answered by agreeing; A agrees and adds a signal.',68),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Student: I was charged a late fee for a book I returned on time. Is there any way to contest it? — Librarian: File a short written appeal with your barcode and the return date. We compare it with the date stamped at the drop box, and if the stamp matches, the fee is reversed within a week.','TTS','["By comparing the stamped return date with the account record","By asking the student to return the book again","By charging the fee to the next semester","By cancelling the student account"]','A',1,'The reversal depends on matching the drop-box stamp to the recorded date.',69),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What must the student submit first?','Student: I was charged a late fee for a book I returned on time. Is there any way to contest it? — Librarian: File a short written appeal with your barcode and the return date. We compare it with the date stamped at the drop box, and if the stamp matches, the fee is reversed within a week.','TTS','["A copy of the book''s title page","The written appeal with the barcode and return date","The professor''s signature","A photo of the book"]','B',1,'The librarian''s first instruction is a short written appeal including barcode and return date.',70),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. How long does a successful appeal take?','Student: I was charged a late fee for a book I returned on time. Is there any way to contest it? — Librarian: File a short written appeal with your barcode and the return date. We compare it with the date stamped at the drop box, and if the stamp matches, the fee is reversed within a week.','TTS','["Immediately","About one week","One full month","At the end of the term"]','B',1,'A reversed fee appears within a week of a matching stamp.',71),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question.','Maya: Our half of the presentation is not ready, and I think we should ask for a new date. — Professor: A one-week extension is possible, but the whole group must appear together after class and agree to shorten the talk to eight minutes.','TTS','["The group must ask in person and accept a shorter talk","Maya may ask alone by email","The professor will postpone the whole exam","The talk must be lengthened instead"]','A',1,'Both conditions are stated: the whole group appears after class, and the talk becomes eight minutes.',72),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. How long is the extension?','Maya: Our half of the presentation is not ready, and I think we should ask for a new date. — Professor: A one-week extension is possible, but the whole group must appear together after class and agree to shorten the talk to eight minutes.','TTS','["One day","One week","One month","Two semesters"]','B',1,'The professor offers a one-week extension.',73),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. Why does Maya visit the professor?','Maya: Our half of the presentation is not ready, and I think we should ask for a new date. — Professor: A one-week extension is possible, but the whole group must appear together after class and agree to shorten the talk to eight minutes.','TTS','["To complain about a grade","To request a new date for her group''s talk","To borrow a laptop","To register for the course"]','B',1,'Maya proposes asking for a new date, which is the purpose of the conversation.',74),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What condition must the visitor meet?','Visitor: Is it possible to use the bench outside class hours? — Technician: Weekend access is allowed for approved projects, but you must bring your supervisor''s signed form and the bench is locked from eleven at night.','TTS','["Paying a weekend fee","Bringing a signed form from a supervisor","Finishing the project in one day","Attending the safety lecture"]','B',1,'Weekend use requires the supervisor''s signed form.',75),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. Until what time may the bench be used?','Visitor: Is it possible to use the bench outside class hours? — Technician: Weekend access is allowed for approved projects, but you must bring your supervisor''s signed form and the bench is locked from eleven at night.','TTS','["Six in the evening","Nine in the evening","Eleven at night","Midnight exactly"]','C',1,'Access ends when the bench is locked at eleven at night.',76),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What does the technician mainly do?','Visitor: Is it possible to use the bench outside class hours? — Technician: Weekend access is allowed for approved projects, but you must bring your supervisor''s signed form and the bench is locked from eleven at night.','TTS','["Explain a rule about equipment access","Grade a laboratory report","Sell a membership card","Book a lecture hall"]','A',1,'The exchange sets out the conditions for using the bench, so the technician explains an access rule.',77),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What will the adviser do?','Student: The attendance list says I missed six classes, but I was at every one of them. — Adviser: Then this is a clerical error, and I will ask the office to reissue the list; the deadline to object is Friday, so bring any proof you have.','TTS','["Reissue the attendance list after checking with the office","Excuse the absences permanently","Cancel the course","Extend the deadline by a month"]','A',1,'The adviser will have the office reissue the list because the record appears wrong.',78),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What must the student do before Friday?','Student: The attendance list says I missed six classes, but I was at every one of them. — Adviser: Then this is a clerical error, and I will ask the office to reissue the list; the deadline to object is Friday, so bring any proof you have.','TTS','["Pay a fine","Bring whatever proof exists","Attend six extra classes","Register for the next term"]','B',1,'The adviser asks the student to bring proof before the Friday objection deadline.',79),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_CONVERSATION','Listen to the conversation and answer the question. What problem does the student raise?','Student: The attendance list says I missed six classes, but I was at every one of them. — Adviser: Then this is a clerical error, and I will ask the office to reissue the list; the deadline to object is Friday, so bring any proof you have.','TTS','["A wrong attendance record","A lost assignment","A closed library","A missing textbook"]','A',1,'The student disputes the attendance record, which the adviser calls a clerical error.',80),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. Where is the temporary shuttle stop?','Attention students. From this Saturday the number 7 shuttle will not stop at the north gate because the road is being resurfaced. Riders should use the temporary stop beside the sports hall instead, and the timetable on that shelter adds five minutes to every trip. Normal service returns on Monday morning.','TTS','["At the north gate","Beside the sports hall","At the main library","On the university square"]','B',1,'The announcement directs riders to the temporary stop beside the sports hall.',81),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. What happens to the journey time?','Attention students. From this Saturday the number 7 shuttle will not stop at the north gate because the road is being resurfaced. Riders should use the temporary stop beside the sports hall instead, and the timetable on that shelter adds five minutes to every trip. Normal service returns on Monday morning.','TTS','["It becomes five minutes longer","It becomes five minutes shorter","It does not change","It doubles for a week"]','A',1,'The temporary timetable adds five minutes per trip.',82),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. When does the temporary arrangement end?','Attention students. From this Saturday the number 7 shuttle will not stop at the north gate because the road is being resurfaced. Riders should use the temporary stop beside the sports hall instead, and the timetable on that shelter adds five minutes to every trip. Normal service returns on Monday morning.','TTS','["On Monday morning","On the following Saturday","At the end of the term","After the road opens in a month"]','A',1,'Normal service resumes Monday morning.',83),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. Who receives a printed copy of the slides?','Good afternoon. For tonight''s guest lecture, seating opens at six in the great hall, and the first two hundred arrivals receive a printed copy of the slides; the rest will follow the talk on the campus channel. Please do not photograph the speaker during the demonstration, as was requested in the programme.','TTS','["Every registered student","The first two hundred arrivals","The speaker''s assistants","Only the front row"]','B',1,'Printed slides go to the first two hundred arrivals.',84),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. What may later arrivals do instead?','Good afternoon. For tonight''s guest lecture, seating opens at six in the great hall, and the first two hundred arrivals receive a printed copy of the slides; the rest will follow the talk on the campus channel. Please do not photograph the speaker during the demonstration, as was requested in the programme.','TTS','["Watch on the campus channel","Leave and return tomorrow","Ask for a refund","Sit in the corridor"]','A',1,'Those without slides follow the talk on the campus channel.',85),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. What is the audience asked not to do?','Good afternoon. For tonight''s guest lecture, seating opens at six in the great hall, and the first two hundred arrivals receive a printed copy of the slides; the rest will follow the talk on the campus channel. Please do not photograph the speaker during the demonstration, as was requested in the programme.','TTS','["Ask questions","Photograph the demonstration","Bring printed notes","Record the channel"]','B',1,'Photographing the speaker during the demonstration is forbidden.',86),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. What happens to a contract returned late?','Housing office notice. The window for returning next year''s room contract closes at eight p.m. on the fifteenth, and a late return will place you on the waiting list instead of your preferred room. Families may view rooms on the weekend of the eighteenth by booking online, and the office will not answer telephone requests that week.','TTS','["It is accepted with a fee","The student joins the waiting list","The room is kept for a week","The family must attend in person"]','B',1,'Late returns lose the preferred room and go to the waiting list.',87),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. How must a viewing be arranged?','Housing office notice. The window for returning next year''s room contract closes at eight p.m. on the fifteenth, and a late return will place you on the waiting list instead of your preferred room. Families may view rooms on the weekend of the eighteenth by booking online, and the office will not answer telephone requests that week.','TTS','["By booking online","By telephoning the office","By writing a letter","By arriving at the door"]','A',1,'Viewings require an online booking; the office will not take telephone requests that week.',88),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_ANNOUNCEMENT','Listen to the announcement and answer the question. What is the purpose of the notice?','Housing office notice. The window for returning next year''s room contract closes at eight p.m. on the fifteenth, and a late return will place you on the waiting list instead of your preferred room. Families may view rooms on the weekend of the eighteenth by booking online, and the office will not answer telephone requests that week.','TTS','["To announce a housing deadline and viewing rules","To advertise a new dormitory","To report a repair","To introduce a housing officer"]','A',1,'The notice states a contract deadline and how weekend viewings work.',89),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. What does the professor say most students choose?','Psychology lecture. Imagine you must remember fifty words. Would you study the list twice in one sitting, or spread the same amount of study time over four days? Students almost always choose the single session because it feels fluent, yet testing shows the opposite. Information rehearsed after a delay must be rebuilt from memory each time, and that act of rebuilding strengthens the trace, whereas rereading a list you have just seen feels easy but adds almost nothing. The practical advice from decades of spacing studies is blunt: study less often and more times, and accept that the harder session is the one that works.','TTS','["Studying the whole list in one sitting","Studying across four days","Testing before studying","Rereading aloud"]','A',1,'Students prefer one sitting because it feels fluent, even though spacing performs better.',90),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. Why does spaced practice strengthen memory according to the lecture?','Psychology lecture. Imagine you must remember fifty words. Would you study the list twice in one sitting, or spread the same amount of study time over four days? Students almost always choose the single session because it feels fluent, yet testing shows the opposite. Information rehearsed after a delay must be rebuilt from memory each time, and that act of rebuilding strengthens the trace, whereas rereading a list you have just seen feels easy but adds almost nothing. The practical advice from decades of spacing studies is blunt: study less often and more times, and accept that the harder session is the one that works.','TTS','["Because each delayed rehearsal requires rebuilding the memory","Because it takes less total time","Because rereading is impossible","Because lists are short"]','A',1,'The rebuilding required after a delay is what strengthens the trace.',91),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','In the talk, what does the word blunt mean?','Psychology lecture. Imagine you must remember fifty words. Would you study the list twice in one sitting, or spread the same amount of study time over four days? Students almost always choose the single session because it feels fluent, yet testing shows the opposite. Information rehearsed after a delay must be rebuilt from memory each time, and that act of rebuilding strengthens the trace, whereas rereading a list you have just seen feels easy but adds almost nothing. The practical advice from decades of spacing studies is blunt: study less often and more times, and accept that the harder session is the one that works.','TTS','["Direct and uncompromising","Confusing and unclear","Old and outdated","Gentle and indirect"]','A',1,'The advice is stated plainly and without softening, so the speaker calls it direct.',92),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. What can be inferred about how learners judge their own studying?','Psychology lecture. Imagine you must remember fifty words. Would you study the list twice in one sitting, or spread the same amount of study time over four days? Students almost always choose the single session because it feels fluent, yet testing shows the opposite. Information rehearsed after a delay must be rebuilt from memory each time, and that act of rebuilding strengthens the trace, whereas rereading a list you have just seen feels easy but adds almost nothing. The practical advice from decades of spacing studies is blunt: study less often and more times, and accept that the harder session is the one that works.','TTS','["Ease during study can suggest mastery that is not there","Learners reliably detect weak memory","Feelings of fluency always predict results","Spacing feels harder only to children"]','A',1,'The lecture contrasts the fluent feeling of one sitting with its poorer result, so ease misleads.',93),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. Why does a bog store carbon so effectively?','Environmental science lecture. Peat bogs cover only a few percent of the land surface, yet they hold more carbon than all the world''s forests. The reason is slow decay. In a waterlogged, acidic, oxygen-poor bog, dead plant material does not rot completely, so year after year a partially preserved layer accumulates and the carbon its plants drew from the air stays locked underground. Drain a bog for farming and air enters the peat; the stored carbon then decomposes and the site turns from a sink into a source, releasing centuries of accumulation in a few decades. Rewetting can halt that loss, but rebuilding peat takes roughly a millimetre per year, which is why conservationists treat an intact bog as something close to irreplaceable.','TTS','["Partial decomposition leaves plant material buried instead of rotted","Its plants grow extremely fast","It is covered by forest","Cold temperatures freeze the carbon"]','A',1,'Waterlogged, acidic, oxygen-poor conditions stop decay, so layers accumulate.',94),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. What happens when a bog is drained?','Environmental science lecture. Peat bogs cover only a few percent of the land surface, yet they hold more carbon than all the world''s forests. The reason is slow decay. In a waterlogged, acidic, oxygen-poor bog, dead plant material does not rot completely, so year after year a partially preserved layer accumulates and the carbon its plants drew from the air stays locked underground. Drain a bog for farming and air enters the peat; the stored carbon then decomposes and the site turns from a sink into a source, releasing centuries of accumulation in a few decades. Rewetting can halt that loss, but rebuilding peat takes roughly a millimetre per year, which is why conservationists treat an intact bog as something close to irreplaceable.','TTS','["It begins releasing stored carbon","It stores carbon faster","Its peat thickens","Its acidity rises further"]','A',1,'Air entering drained peat causes decomposition, turning the bog from sink to source.',95),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','Listen to the academic talk and answer the question. Why does the professor call an intact bog close to irreplaceable?','Environmental science lecture. Peat bogs cover only a few percent of the land surface, yet they hold more carbon than all the world''s forests. The reason is slow decay. In a waterlogged, acidic, oxygen-poor bog, dead plant material does not rot completely, so year after year a partially preserved layer accumulates and the carbon its plants drew from the air stays locked underground. Drain a bog for farming and air enters the peat; the stored carbon then decomposes and the site turns from a sink into a source, releasing centuries of accumulation in a few decades. Rewetting can halt that loss, but rebuilding peat takes roughly a millimetre per year, which is why conservationists treat an intact bog as something close to irreplaceable.','TTS','["Peat rebuilds at only about a millimetre per year","Draining is illegal everywhere","Rewetting is impossible","Bogs cannot be restored at all"]','A',1,'Recovery after rewetting is extremely slow, so lost peat cannot practically be rebuilt.',96),
(@set02_exam_id,'LISTENING','LISTEN_TALK','In the talk, what does the phrase sink into a source describe?','Environmental science lecture. Peat bogs cover only a few percent of the land surface, yet they hold more carbon than all the world''s forests. The reason is slow decay. In a waterlogged, acidic, oxygen-poor bog, dead plant material does not rot completely, so year after year a partially preserved layer accumulates and the carbon its plants drew from the air stays locked underground. Drain a bog for farming and air enters the peat; the stored carbon then decomposes and the site turns from a sink into a source, releasing centuries of accumulation in a few decades. Rewetting can halt that loss, but rebuilding peat takes roughly a millimetre per year, which is why conservationists treat an intact bog as something close to irreplaceable.','TTS','["From storing carbon to releasing it","From wet ground to dry ground","From forest to farmland","From fast to slow decay"]','A',1,'A carbon sink absorbs carbon; a source emits it, so the phrase marks that reversal.',97);
INSERT INTO tp_questions (exam_id, section, task_type, prompt, body_text, audio_mode, choices_json, answer_key, auto_score, explanation, order_no) VALUES
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["The","results","were","not","published","by","the","team"]','the results were not published by the team',1,'Passive declarative order: subject (The results) + verb phrase (were not published) + agent (by the team).',98),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Every","attendee","must","show","a","valid","badge","at","the","door"]','every attendee must show a valid badge at the door',1,'Subject (Every attendee) + modal verb phrase (must show) + object (a valid badge) + place (at the door).',99),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["She","has","been","reviewing","applications","since","Monday"]','she has been reviewing applications since monday',1,'Present perfect continuous: She + has been reviewing + object + time since Monday.',100),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Although","the","sample","was","small","the","trend","was","clear"]','although the sample was small the trend was clear',1,'Subordinate clause first (Although the sample was small), then the main clause (the trend was clear).',101),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["We","should","ask","the","librarian","to","extend","the","reservation"]','we should ask the librarian to extend the reservation',1,'Subject + modal (should ask) + indirect object (the librarian) + infinitive complement (to extend the reservation).',102),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Not","only","the","method","but","also","the","timing","matters"]','not only the method but also the timing matters',1,'The correlative pair not only … but also … must join the two nouns in that order.',103),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["The","committee","decided","to","postpone","the","vote","until","April"]','the committee decided to postpone the vote until april',1,'Subject (The committee) + verb (decided) + infinitive (to postpone the vote) + time (until April).',104),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','BUILD_SENTENCE','Put the words in the correct order to form one grammatical sentence.',NULL,'NONE','["Had","the","data","been","cleaner","the","model","would","have","worked"]','had the data been cleaner the model would have worked',1,'Inverted third conditional: Had the data been cleaner, then the model would have worked.',105),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to your course coordinator (about 50-80 words) explaining that you were absent from Tuesday''s practical because of a medical appointment, and ask whether a make-up session can be arranged. Greet the coordinator and sign your name.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual tutor review. TestPrep does not auto-score writing. A strong answer states the reason, asks a specific question, and closes politely.',106),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_EMAIL','Write an email to the library director (about 50-80 words) requesting that two study rooms be bookable for whole weeks rather than single sessions. Explain the problem with the current rule and suggest one workable change.',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual tutor review. Aim for one clear request, one reason, and one concrete suggestion in a polite register.',107),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Professor: Some universities ask first-year students to take one course outside their main field. Claire thinks this wastes time that should go to the major; Daniel thinks it broadens students usefully. State your own opinion and support it with a reason or example (about 100 words).',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual tutor review. Give a position, one developed reason, and ideally a specific example; you may agree with or oppose either student.',108),
(@set02_exam_id,'WRITING','WRITE_DISCUSSION','Professor: Campus cafeterias increasingly print the carbon cost of each dish. Priya believes the label will change habits; Marcus believes students ignore such labels and only price matters. Explain which view you agree with more and why (about 100 words).',NULL,'NONE',NULL,NULL,0,'Saved for manual tutor review. Compare the two views and defend your choice with evidence or reasoning.',109);
